# Project Specification (`.project/spec.yaml`)

The `spec.yaml` file is the main configuration for AI Workbench projects. It defines the container environment, applications, resources, and more that aren't represented with existing common files.

## Project Metadata

```yaml
meta:
  name: my-name             # Project name
  image: project-my-name    # Docker image name when built locally
  defaultBranch: main       # Default git branch
```

## Layout Section

The `layout` section in spec.yaml controls git-lfs and gitignore patterns. **Don't edit this directly** — instead, modify `.gitattributes` and `.gitignore` files directly as you normally would.

## Base Environment (`environment.base`)

**Don't edit this directly - it is useful to understand the container's contents**

Section that contains information about the base container (the `FROM` image):

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| `registry` | Container registry (e.g., `nvcr.io`) |
| `image` | Base image path and tag |
| `name` | Human-readable name for the base environment |
| `cuda_version` | CUDA version installed (e.g., `12.0`). **Important for GPU work - THIS IS THE VERSION OF CUDA IN THE CONTAINER** |
| `entrypoint_script` | Path to custom entrypoint script that is exec'd inside the AI Workbench entrypoint script (usually empty) |

### User Info (`environment.base.user_info`)

Indicates if the base container has a specific non-root user that should be used:

```yaml
user_info:
  uid: "1001"
  gid: "1000"
  username: rapids
```

If empty, AI Workbench creates a non-root user (`workbench`) matching host permissions so bind mounts work correctly. If it is set, AI Workbench handles making UID/GID match by either launching the container with the appropriate args or remapping permissions.

### Package Managers (`environment.base.package_managers`)

Shows available package managers in the container and pre-installed packages:

```yaml
package_managers:
  - name: apt
    binary_path: /usr/bin/apt
    installed_packages: [curl, git, python3, ...]
  - name: pip
    binary_path: /usr/bin/pip
    installed_packages: [jupyterlab==4.3.5]
```

**Note**: What packages are listed as `installed_packages` is informational and not always accurate — always inspect the container directly (e.g., `pip freeze`) for accurate installed packages.

### Package Manager Environment

If the base container was built using a conda or virtual environments:

```yaml
package_manager_environment:
  name: "conda"         # "conda" or "venv"
  target: "/opt/conda"  # Path to environment
```

### Compose File Path

For multi-container environments, AI Workbench can start Docker Compose services alongside the project container. See `.cursor/rules/ai-workbench/compose.mdc` for rules if multiple containers are needed.

AI Workbench supports a handful of default paths but you can specify a custom one:

```yaml
compose_file_path: "my-app/docker-compose.yml"  # Path to compose file (usually empty)
```

## Applications (`execution.apps`)

Apps defined here are merged with any apps pre-installed in the base environment (`environment.base.apps`). Apps can be added without rebuilding.

Three classes:

### Native Apps (**do not modify**)

Managed by AI Workbench for Cursor/VS Code connectivity:

```yaml
- name: cursor
  type: cursor
  class: native
```

### Process Apps

Background processes (e.g. downloads, data processing, etc.):

```yaml
- name: Download Models
  type: custom
  class: process
  start_command: /bin/bash ./download.sh # Command that is executed
  health_check_command: pgrep -f "^/bin/bash ./download.sh" >/dev/null # returns true if running
  stop_command: pkill -f "^/bin/bash ./download.sh" # command to stop the process
  user_msg: "Downloading model files..." # message to display to the end user when starting
  logfile_path: ""           # If set, streams this file; otherwise captures stdout
  timeout_seconds: 3600 # time to complete the process or an error is raised
  process_options:
    wait_until_finished: true  # If true, UI waits for completion
```

### Web Apps

Services routed through Traefik proxy for browser access (e.g. web apps, APIs, etc.):

```yaml
- name: inference
  type: custom
  class: webapp
  start_command: python3 -m myapp # Command that is executed
  health_check_command: curl -f "http://localhost:8080/" # returns true if running & ideally healthy/ready
  stop_command: pkill -f "^python3 -m myapp" # command to stop the process
  user_msg: "" # message to display to the end user when starting
  timeout_seconds: 120 # time for the web app to become healthy/start
  logfile_path: ""           # If set, UI streams this file; otherwise captures stdout
  webapp_options:
    autolaunch: true          # Auto-open browser when healthy
    port: "8080"              # Local port to route
    proxy:
      trim_prefix: true       # Remove URL prefix before forwarding
    url: http://localhost:8080/        # Static URL (use this OR url_command)
    url_command: jupyter lab list ...  # Command to get URL (use this OR url)
```

When adding a web app it must be configured to run behind the reverse proxy. The `PROXY_PREFIX` env var is available in the `start_command` to provide the url prefix to an app.

**CRITICAL: Environment Variable Inheritance in start_command**

When using pipes (`|`) or subshells in `start_command`, environment variables like `PROXY_PREFIX` may not be inherited. You must explicitly pass them:

```yaml
# ❌ WRONG - PROXY_PREFIX won't be available to the Python process
start_command: python3 app.py 2>&1 | tee app.log

# ✅ CORRECT - Explicitly pass the variable
start_command: PROXY_PREFIX=$PROXY_PREFIX python3 app.py 2>&1 | tee app.log
```

**CRITICAL: url requires a trailing slash** The `url` & `url_command` fields must have a trailing slash to route through the proxy properly (e.g. http://localhost:8080/ )

## Resources (`execution.resources`)

```yaml
resources:
  gpu:
    requested: 1           # Number of GPUs (0 = no GPU)
  sharedMemoryMB: 1024     # Size of /dev/shm in MB
```

`resources.gpu.requested` is the number of GPUs that will be passed from the host to the project container.

**IMPORTANT**: You are running in the container and nvidia-smi will return only what is passed to the container. You may need to ask the user how many GPUs their system has if you need to change `resources.gpu.requested` or understand what is possible.

## Secrets (`execution.secrets`)

For sensitive environment variables (API keys, tokens). AI Workbench prompts users for values before container start so only the key needs to be configured:

```yaml
secrets:
  - variable: HF_TOKEN
    description: HuggingFace API token
  - variable: OPENAI_API_KEY
    description: OpenAI API key
```

**NEVER USE VARIABLES.ENV FOR SENSITIVE VALUES. ALWAYS USE SECRETS**

To add a secret:
1. Add entry to `execution.secrets` in spec.yaml
2. Restart the container
3. AI Workbench will prompt the user for the secret value on start

## Mounts (`execution.mounts`)

You configure additional mounts to persist data in the container:

```yaml
mounts:
  - type: project          # Always present - the /project bind mount - DON'T EDIT THIS
    target: /project/
    description: Project directory
    options: rw

  - type: volume           # Docker/Podman managed volume 
    target: /mnt/cache/
    description: Persistent cache storage
    options: ""

  - type: host             # Bind mount from host (user prompted for source)
    target: /mnt/data/
    description: Local data directory
    options: ""

  - type: tmp              # Temporary mount
    target: /mnt/scratch/
    description: Fast scratch space
    options: ""
```

**Requires container restart** after modifying mounts.

Prefer volume mounts over host mounts where it makes sense because no user interaction is required. Only use host mounts if the data truly needs to share with the host file system easily.